`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"A sweep is many small scheduler jobs",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Week 6 gave you the strategies for a run that needs more than one device: distributed data parallel (DDP) and sharding. A scaling sweep needs them less than you might expect. Most of its points are small training runs, far below the size that needs more than one device. Each goes to the cluster's scheduler as a job of its own, and the scheduler runs many such jobs in parallel. Within-run DDP or sharding comes in only for a job that needs more than one GPU."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"That machinery does not tell you which size to train, how many tokens to give it, or whether the trained model is any good."})]})]})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(l,{tab:"Loss Follows Laws",plate:"hooke-capillary-tubes",children:[e.jsxs(n,{title:"Loss follows power laws in N, D, C",figure:e.jsx(m,{view:"sweep",showBudgetLine:!1}),figureWidth:"1/2",accent:7,number:"7",children:[e.jsx(a,{math:"L(N) \\approx \\left(\\tfrac{N_c}{N}\\right)^{\\alpha_N}, \\quad L(D) \\approx \\left(\\tfrac{D_c}{D}\\right)^{\\alpha_D}, \\quad L(C) \\approx \\left(\\tfrac{C_c}{C}\\right)^{\\alpha_C}",label:"(1)"}),e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Kaplan et al.: loss against N, D, C ",e.jsx(s,{id:"kaplan2020",children:"Kaplan et al., 2020"})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(f,{name:"scaling law",children:"Power law"}),": straight on log-log axes"]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"N_c, D_c, C_c fitted; α: the slope"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Plot: synthetic sweep, one curve per N_total"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Curves flatten at a floor the law omits"}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"A power law is a straight line on log-log axes",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Kaplan et al. trained Transformer language models over a wide range of sizes and plotted the test loss against each of the three quantities. Across the ranges they measured, each relation is close to a straight line on log-log axes ",e.jsx(s,{id:"kaplan2020",children:"Kaplan et al., 2020"}),". Equation (1) is an empirical fit, not a law derived from first principles, and it describes the loss only over the ranges Kaplan et al. studied. A fitted power law of this kind, which predicts loss from scale, is what we call a scaling law."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Each form holds when the resource it names is the bottleneck and the others are abundant. The first form, ",e.jsx(a,{math:"L(N)",display:!1,trail:","})," describes models trained on enough data that data does not limit them. The second, ",e.jsx(a,{math:"L(D)",display:!1,trail:","})," describes models large enough that size does not limit them, and the third, ",e.jsx(a,{math:"L(C)",display:!1,trail:","})," describes runs that spend their compute efficiently."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Why a straight line? Take the logarithm of the first form. The logarithm turns the power into a product and the quotient into a difference:"}),e.jsx(a,{math:"\\log L = \\alpha_N \\log N_c - \\alpha_N \\log N."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Plotted against ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\log N",display:!1,trail:","})," this is a line with slope ",e.jsx(a,{math:"-\\alpha_N",display:!1,trail:"."})," The fitted constant ",e.jsx(a,{math:"N_c",display:!1})," only sets its height. The same step works for D and C. A straight line is easy to fit and easy to extend, which is why scaling fits are made on log-log axes."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The step needs (1) as written. A full description of the loss adds a constant term, the irreducible loss that more scale cannot remove. Equation (1) leaves it out, as Kaplan et al.'s fits over their range do. With the constant added, ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\log L",display:!1})," is no longer linear in ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\log N",display:!1,trail:","})," and the curve flattens as the power-law term shrinks toward zero."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The fitted exponents are small. For parameters ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\alpha_N \\approx 0.076",display:!1,trail:","})," and for data ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\alpha_D \\approx 0.095",display:!1,trail:"."})," Compute has the smallest, ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\alpha_C \\approx 0.050",display:!1,trail:"."})," A small exponent means that loss falls slowly with scale. Under ",e.jsx(a,{math:"L(C) \\propto C^{-0.05}",display:!1,trail:","})," ten times more compute multiplies the modeled loss term by only ",e.jsx(a,{math:"10^{-0.05} \\approx 0.89",display:!1,trail:","})," an 11% reduction."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Kaplan et al. count N as ",e.jsx(a,{math:"N_\\text{nonemb}",display:!1,trail:","})," the parameters outside the vocabulary and positional embeddings. The plot's legend counts ",e.jsx(a,{math:"N_\\text{total}",display:!1,trail:","})," every parameter including the tied output head (the unembedding), the output projection that shares its weight matrix with the input embedding."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The plot shows a sweep of the kind such a fit starts from: loss against training tokens D on log-log axes, one curve per model size. Every curve falls as its model sees more tokens. The curves bend although a power law is straight, because they include the floor that equation (1) leaves out. With the model size fixed and D growing, the loss approaches the irreducible loss plus that size's own term, the loss its limited size costs. Larger models therefore lie lower and flatten at a lower level. All curves come from one parametric loss model, so the plot shows the shape of a sweep, without the noise of real runs."})]})]}),e.jsxs(n,{reading:!0,title:"Three regimes, one curve",accent:0,number:"8",items:"3",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Small data: noisy, before the law holds"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Power-law region: the straight middle"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Irreducible error: a fitted floor ",e.jsx(s,{id:"hestness2017",children:"Hestness et al., 2017"})]}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"A loss curve has three regions, and the power law is the middle one",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A single loss curve has three regimes, each a stretch of the curve ",e.jsx(s,{id:"hestness2017",children:"Hestness et al., 2017"}),". In the ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"small-data region"}),", the model has seen too little data for the fitted scaling relation to hold, and its loss is data-limited and noisy. In the ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"power-law region"}),", the curve is approximately a straight line on log-log axes, and (1) describes it. In the ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"irreducible-error region"}),", the curve approaches an empirical floor under the fixed data distribution and objective. The floor belongs to that distribution and that objective. Change the training data or what the model is trained to predict, and the floor can move."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Why should the middle be a power law at all? An illustrative analogy is mean estimation. Estimate a mean from ",e.jsx(a,{math:"n",display:!1})," samples with variance ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\sigma^2",display:!1,trail:","})," and the squared error of the estimate falls like ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\sigma^2/n",display:!1,trail:"."})," Use that estimate to predict a fresh sample, and the expected squared error is ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\sigma^2 + \\sigma^2/n",display:!1,trail:"."})," The second term falls as a power of ",e.jsx(a,{math:"n",display:!1,trail:","})," and the first is a floor that no amount of data removes. The analogy shows only this shape. It derives no neural scaling law, and its mechanism does not carry over. Its exponent is 1, while the language-model exponents are below 0.1. The fitted floor is a constant estimated by a fit, and without further assumptions it is not the entropy of the data."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(n,{title:"Compare slopes and offsets, not single runs",figure:e.jsx(k,{}),figureWidth:"1/2",accent:1,number:"9",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"One run: the winner at that scale"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"A fitted line: the winner at your target"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Two lines can cross before the target ",e.jsx(s,{id:"kaplan2020",children:"Kaplan et al., 2020"})]}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"A fitted line decides between designs at the target scale",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A scaling law turns one-off comparisons into engineering decisions. You compare two architectures or two optimizers by the slope and offset of their fitted lines, because the line extends to the scale you cannot afford to run. The offset summarizes the difference at a reference scale, and the exponent summarizes how fast loss falls. A decision for the target scale needs both."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The figure is an illustrative case. Both axes are logarithmic, so each design's fitted power law is a straight line. Design A starts lower, and design B falls more steeply. Look at where the two lines cross: to the right of the shaded band of runs you can afford, and to the left of the dashed target. Every run in the band ranks A first, yet the fits predict that B wins at the target. A comparison of single runs would have picked the wrong design."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Kaplan et al. give an example. In their tested setting, Transformers and LSTMs differ in how their loss scales on tokens late in the context, and extrapolated curves can identify a likely crossover before a run at the target scale ",e.jsx(s,{id:"kaplan2020",children:"Kaplan et al., 2020"}),". Such a forecast reaches beyond the fitted range, so a held-out run there has to confirm the crossover before you rely on it."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(n,{title:"Counting convention bends small-scale fits",accent:2,number:"10",items:"5",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Kaplan: non-embedding parameters and compute"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Course: N_total, every parameter counted"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Embedding share shrinks as models grow"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Straight in one convention, bent in the other ",e.jsx(s,{id:"pearce2024",children:"Pearce & Song, 2024"})]}),`
`]}),e.jsx(d,{type:"warning",children:e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Exercise: fit under both conventions"})}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"The counting convention bends fits at small scale",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The convention you pick for counting N and C changes the fit. At the large end of the scale the difference hardly matters, because the embedding matrix is a small share of a large model. In the student range it is a large share, and that share changes with the model's size. It shrinks as the model grows, because the embedding grows only with the width, while the Transformer blocks grow with width and depth."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Compute shows the same pattern. The unembedding matmul, which turns each final hidden state into a score for every vocabulary entry, is a large share of a small model's FLOPs. The input lookup costs no matmul FLOPs, because it only selects one row of the embedding matrix."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Why does a changing share bend a line? On a log axis, switching conventions moves each point by the logarithm of the ratio between its two counts. That ratio is large for small models and close to 1 for large ones, so small models move further than large ones. Points that lie on a straight line in one convention therefore lie on a curve in the other. Pearce and Song show that this choice visibly bends fits at small scale ",e.jsx(s,{id:"pearce2024",children:"Pearce & Song, 2024"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The exercise computes C from total FLOPs per token, and it uses both conventions. You fit the same sweep once under each and watch the fitted exponent move. Week 2's coarse estimate of training compute, ",e.jsx(a,{math:"C \\approx 6ND",display:!1,trail:","})," inherits whichever N you put into it, so state which convention is active every time you write it."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Even with consistent accounting, a smooth loss does not guarantee a smooth downstream metric, meaning a task score such as benchmark accuracy as opposed to the loss ",e.jsx(s,{id:"tay2022",children:"Tay et al., 2022"}),"."]})]})]})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(l,{tab:"Chinchilla",plate:"encyclopedie-balancier-romaines",children:[e.jsxs(n,{title:"At a fixed budget, N and D trade off",accent:3,number:"11",children:[e.jsx(a,{math:"C \\approx 6\\,N\\,D \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\text{fix } C,\\ \\text{choose } (N, D)\\ \\text{to minimize } L",label:"(2)"}),e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"C ≈ 6ND: a proxy for FLOPs"}),`
