`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Toggle the exponent: the forecast swings"}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Llama 3 extrapolated its fit about 3,800 times, where the exponent decides the size",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The forecast that opened the lecture is this playbook run at scale. Meta fitted a parabola to each sweep budget's losses to find its compute-optimal model. It then fitted how the optimal number of training tokens grows with compute, and the parameter count follows from the budget. The fit was extrapolated about 3,800 times beyond the largest sweep budget, to the flagship run's ",e.jsx(a,{math:"3.8 \\times 10^{25}",display:!1})," FLOPs. It forecast about 402B parameters trained on 16.55T tokens. The chosen 405B parameters on 15.6T tokens lie near that reported optimum ",e.jsx(s,{id:"llama3-2024",children:"Grattafiori et al., 2024"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Extrapolation amplifies differences in a fitted exponent. A generic illustration with model-size exponents, not Llama 3's own fit, shows how much. Suppose the optimal size grows as ",e.jsx(a,{math:"N^*(C) \\propto C^{a}",display:!1,trail:"."})," A budget ",e.jsx(a,{math:"r",display:!1})," times larger then multiplies the optimal size by ",e.jsx(a,{math:"r^{a}",display:!1,trail:"."})," Two exponents that differ by ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\Delta a",display:!1})," therefore disagree on the size by a factor of ",e.jsx(a,{math:"r^{\\Delta a}",display:!1,trail:","})," which grows with the distance ",e.jsx(a,{math:"r",display:!1,trail:"."})," Start Kaplan's 0.73 and Chinchilla's 0.50 from the same size at the top sweep budget, and extrapolate 3,800 times:"]}),e.jsx(a,{math:"3800^{0.73} \\approx 410 \\quad\\text{against}\\quad 3800^{0.50} \\approx 62 ."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The two recommended sizes end up about 6.7 times apart (our arithmetic). The exercise extrapolates only about 10 times, where the same two exponents differ by a factor of ",e.jsx(a,{math:"10^{0.23} \\approx 1.7",display:!1,trail:"."})," That is still large, and the exercise requires validation against the held-out reference run."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The widget fits the same synthetic sweep as the other views, with budgets from ",e.jsx(a,{math:"10^{16}",display:!1})," to ",e.jsx(a,{math:"10^{20}",display:!1})," FLOPs. Its points are not Llama 3's sweep. Each point is one budget's compute-optimal size ",e.jsx(a,{math:"N^*",display:!1,trail:"."})," The solid line is the power law fitted to these points. Its exponent is 0.50 by construction, because the synthetic loss model was built with that allocation, so it is not Hoffmann et al.'s estimate, although the widget labels it Chinchilla. The dashed line projects ",e.jsx(a,{math:"N^*(C)",display:!1})," from the middle budget, ",e.jsx(a,{math:"10^{18}",display:!1})," FLOPs, with the exponent you select. With Kaplan's 0.73 it is steeper than the fitted line and moves away from the dots."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The vertical line only marks where Llama 3's budget lies on the compute axis. Both projections start at ",e.jsx(a,{math:"10^{18}",display:!1})," FLOPs, so they reach that line after a factor of ",e.jsx(a,{math:"3.8 \\times 10^{7}",display:!1})," in compute. There they end ",e.jsx(a,{math:"(3.8 \\times 10^{7})^{0.23} \\approx 55",display:!1})," times apart (our arithmetic). The 6.7 above belongs to Llama 3's own extrapolation of 3,800 times, which is far shorter. Toggle the exponent and watch how far the projected ",e.jsx(a,{math:"N^*",display:!1})," moves at the right edge."]})]})]}),e.jsx(y,{caption:"With the exponent at 0.50, the dashed projection leaves the middle budget on a flatter slope than the slide's 0.73 and ends far lower at the right edge, beyond the Llama 3 budget line. The exponent moves the recommended model size most at the largest budgets.",children:e.jsx(m,{view:"fit",initialExponent:"chinchilla"})}),e.jsxs(n,{title:"Your exercise runs this playbook in miniature",accent:6,number:"21",items:"4",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Sweep: five small sizes, WSD branches"}),`
