`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Bet: predict a run ten times larger"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Measure: lm-eval harness, two setup ablations"}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Each step of the playbook is a deliverable of the exercise",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Sweep."})," You train about five model sizes, from about 2M to 50M parameters, and the width rule gives every size the same shape. Each size gets one WSD stable-prefix run and four decay branches, each costing about 10% of its completed endpoint. The 25 jobs are launched as array jobs on the cluster's scheduler. All stable runs and branches together must fit within the course's one-hour limit for a main run. The grid of sizes must bracket the IsoFLOP minima, so that every minimum is interior. The exercise's ledger records the compute of every run and recomputes the WSD saving for your endpoints."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Fit."})," Each run's compute is ",e.jsx(a,{math:"C = F \\cdot D",display:!1,trail:"."})," Here ",e.jsx(a,{math:"F",display:!1})," is the counted FLOPs per token of one forward and backward pass, attention and the tied unembedding included. The ledger records the coarse proxy ",e.jsx(a,{math:"6ND",display:!1})," beside it as a check, but the budgets use the counted value. A budget is one value of ",e.jsx(a,{math:"C",display:!1,trail:","})," and a model of size ",e.jsx(a,{math:"N",display:!1})," meets it at ",e.jsx(a,{math:"D = C/F(N)",display:!1})," tokens."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Your decay branches end at four token counts, which rarely fall exactly on that ",e.jsx(a,{math:"D",display:!1,trail:"."})," A provided helper therefore interpolates each size's final loss between its endpoints and reads it off at ",e.jsx(a,{math:"D = C/F(N)",display:!1,trail:"."})," That is how Chinchilla's Approach 1 reads training curves at fixed compute."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Per budget you fit the IsoFLOP quadratic in ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\log N",display:!1})," to the raw losses, with ",e.jsx(a,{math:"a",display:!1})," its quadratic and ",e.jsx(a,{math:"b",display:!1})," its linear coefficient. Its vertex, ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\log N^* = -b/(2a)",display:!1,trail:","})," is the minimum only when ",e.jsx(a,{math:"a > 0",display:!1,trail:"."})," A fit with ",e.jsx(a,{math:"a \\le 0",display:!1,trail:","})," or with a vertex at or beyond the edge of the trained sizes, is rejected. The minima give ",e.jsx(a,{math:"N^*(C)",display:!1})," and ",e.jsx(a,{math:"D^*(C)",display:!1,trail:"."})," You then fit ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\log N^*",display:!1})," against ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\log C",display:!1})," and report the fit's uncertainty. Because the helper forms IsoFLOP groups from the same runs under either parameter convention, a second grouping under Kaplan's non-embedding convention shows how far the exponent moves."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Bet."})," You extrapolate about 10 times in compute and predict the loss of a lecturer-provided reference model of about 150M parameters. This reference model is the exercise's held-out run. Then you compare the prediction with its actual loss."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Measure."})," A thin wrapper drives lm-eval-harness, an open-source evaluation harness ",e.jsx(s,{id:"gao2023lmeval",children:"Gao et al., 2023"}),". The exercise pins it at v0.4.12 and runs it on the reference base model and on a small off-the-shelf instruct model. It runs a subset of MMLU-Pro, a multiple-choice benchmark, and one further task. Each run records its protocol metadata: the harness version, the task configuration and the dataset revision. You compare one setup ablation per model: 0-shot against 5-shot for the base model, and, for the instruct model, its chat template (the turn formatting it was trained with) on against off."]})]})]})]}),`
