`]}),e.jsx(p,{}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Whether a score is valid depends on the decision it informs",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Before you read a score, ask what the evaluation is for. The four purposes ask different things of the same number. For developer feedback, a proxy that ranks checkpoints during training may tolerate a known bias, as long as the ranking is stable enough to say which run to continue. A purchase decision, about which model to deploy or pay for, needs more. So does a benefit-and-harm claim made for policy or safety. Both need contamination controls, a statement of uncertainty, and complete disclosure of the setup."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Validity is therefore relative to the decision ",e.jsx(s,{id:"hardt-benchmarks",children:"Hardt, 2026"}),". A benchmark that is useful for one purpose can be insufficient for another, so different purposes justify different benchmark designs. Name the decision first, then ask whether the evidence supports it."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The badge keeps LLaMA-65B's three MMLU scores from the previous slide in view. All three measure the same weights, so a number alone does not say which decision it can support. The protocol that produced it has to be reported with it."})]})]}),e.jsxs(n,{reading:!0,title:"A benchmark is one operationalization of good",accent:6,number:"29",items:"4",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Task accuracy: a defined ",e.jsx(f,{name:"benchmark",children:"benchmark"})," task"]}),`
