`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Terminal-Bench 3.0: a 4.9-point gap became 12.7 ",e.jsx(s,{id:"tbench30-2026",children:"Marten et al., 2026a"})]}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Repeated optimization uses up a benchmark's headroom",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Widely optimized public benchmarks often follow a four-stage cycle, shown in the figure from left to right. A benchmark is released as a hard, clearly specified test. The community climbs it. Repeated optimization compresses the headroom until top scores bunch near the ceiling. A new test then takes its place, as MMLU-Pro followed MMLU ",e.jsx(s,{id:"wang2024mmlupro",children:"Wang et al., 2024"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:['The statuses below are as of October 2026. Artificial Analysis maintains an Intelligence Index, a weighted average over a set of benchmarks. In September 2026 its version 4.2 removed GPQA Diamond, "an exceptional scientific reasoning evaluation that has now been saturated" ',e.jsx(s,{id:"aa-index-v42",children:"Artificial Analysis, 2026b"}),". GPQA was built to be hard: PhD experts answered about 65% of the full set correctly ",e.jsx(s,{id:"rein2023gpqa",children:"Rein et al., 2023"}),". MathArena, which evaluates models on new math competitions ",e.jsx(s,{id:"matharena2025",children:"Balunović et al., 2025"}),", now lists final-answer competitions as a deprecated category, the American Invitational Mathematics Examination (AIME) 2026 among them ",e.jsx(s,{id:"matharena-leaderboard",children:"MathArena, n.d."}),'. Its maintainers concluded that "final-answer competition problems are no longer an effective source of hard benchmark tasks for frontier models" ',e.jsx(s,{id:"matharena-nofa2026",children:"Sun et al., 2026"}),". A saturated benchmark stops separating frontier models, while it can still measure models far below its ceiling."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The last stage restores the resolution. On Terminal-Bench 2.1, the best of the compared models, Fable 5 in Claude Code, scored 83.8%. Fable 5 and Opus 4.8, both in Claude Code, were 4.9 points apart on 2.1 and 12.7 points apart on the harder 3.0, released on 2026-07-30 with 74 new tasks ",e.jsx(s,{id:"tbench30-2026",children:"Marten et al., 2026a"}),". On a saturated benchmark, a large difference in capability shows up as a difference of a few tasks. Harder tasks bring the scores back into the range where the benchmark can resolve differences."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(n,{optional:!0,title:"Five questions take any benchmark apart",accent:3,number:"44",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Format, scoring, setup, failure modes, status"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["CritPt: test answers private, on a grading server ",e.jsx(s,{id:"critpt2025",children:"Zhu et al., 2025"})]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"One of CritPt's 70 test items moves the score 1.4 points"}),`
`]}),e.jsx(x,{}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Format, scoring, setup, failure modes and status",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A benchmark score compresses many choices into one number. Five questions take it apart. ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Format"})," asks what an item looks like and what the model must produce. ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Scoring"})," asks how the output becomes a number. ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Setup"})," asks what surrounds the model: the examples in the prompt, the template, the tools, the time limit and the number of trials. ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Failure modes"})," ask what can make the number wrong without anyone cheating, such as fragile answer extraction, contamination, too few items or the agent's scaffold. ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Status"})," asks where the benchmark stands today: current, saturated at the frontier or retired from a leaderboard. A status always carries a date."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The widget applies the template to MMLU-Pro, CritPt and Terminal-Bench 4.0. Each tab shows one item, which answers the format question, and four chips for the other questions. Compare the chips row by row. Scoring moves from a letter that a regular expression extracts from generated chain of thought, to a grading server, to tests in a verifier container. The course wrote every displayed item in its benchmark's format. None is copied from a benchmark."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["CritPt (Complex Research using Integrated Thinking, Physics Test) measures reasoning on unpublished, research-level physics problems, in 71 challenges ",e.jsx(s,{id:"critpt2025",children:"Zhu et al., 2025"}),'. A grading server checks every answer: numbers, SymPy expressions and Python functions. The authors publish one example challenge. The solutions to the other 70, the test set, "are kept private to avoid potential contamination" ',e.jsx(s,{id:"critpt2025",children:"Zhu et al., 2025"}),". Answers that were never published cannot appear in training data. A small set has a price: in an evaluator with n items, one item is worth 100/n percentage points, about 1.4 points for CritPt's 70 challenges. Artificial Analysis lists CritPt with a private test set in its index v4.3 ",e.jsx(s,{id:"aa-index-v43",children:"Artificial Analysis, 2026c"}),"."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(n,{optional:!0,title:"Terminal-Bench 4.0: an agent, a container, a verifier",accent:7,number:"45",items:"4",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
