`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Nanochat's IsoFLOP fits put the optimum near 8 tokens per parameter",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The same fitting procedure can produce a different allocation constant when the recipe and the scale change. Nanochat, a minimal open codebase for training language models, ran the IsoFLOP method on its own recipe ",e.jsx(s,{id:"karpathy2026nanochat-miniseries",children:"Karpathy, 2026a"}),". It fixed the compute of each run with its ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"--target_flops"})," option, swept the model depth, and fitted a quadratic to each IsoFLOP U-curve. Across three budgets, the post prints ratios of 8.1 to 8.5 tokens per parameter at the fitted optima. These ratios count every parameter of the January 2026 recipe. Nanochat's later code re-fitted the rule for a changed architecture and sets 12 tokens per parameter of its transformer matrices and output head, a different denominator ",e.jsx(s,{id:"karpathy2026nanochat",children:"Karpathy, 2026b"}),"."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The figure plots the three fitted optima against training compute on a log scale, at 1 × 10¹⁸, 3 × 10¹⁸ and 6 × 10¹⁸ FLOPs. The vertical axis is the validation bits per byte, which does not depend on the tokenizer. Lower is better. Each point carries its parameter count, its token count and its ratio of tokens to parameters. The bits per byte fall from 0.9781 at the smallest budget to 0.8874 at the largest."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The January 2026 maintainer report used one node of eight H100 GPUs for this miniseries. It suggests that Muon, the small model scale or other nanochat specifics could explain the fitted ratio near 8. Chinchilla's roughly 20 tokens per parameter was fitted with a different recipe, so the two numbers do not conflict. A fitted constant holds for the recipe and the budget range it was measured on."})]})]})]}),`
