`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Choose the model from fitted small runs, and know it worked from a reproducible measurement",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Which model."})," You sweep sizes and token counts at budgets far below the final run's and fit power laws in log space. The IsoFLOP minima give the compute-optimal size and token count at each budget. Chinchilla's minima count training compute alone ",e.jsx(s,{id:"hoffmann2022",children:"Hoffmann et al., 2022"}),". A model that will serve many tokens pays for each of them again, so it is trained past that optimum, as Llama 3 8B and 70B were. You trust a fit at a larger scale only once it predicts a run it was not fitted to. The fit is fair only if the sweep fixes the data, the architecture rule, the schedule and the measurement. The learning rate and the batch size scale by a rule declared in advance. WSD saves compute because all token endpoints of one model size share a single stable prefix, and each adds only its own decay branch."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"How you know."})," Through a measurement you can reproduce. A small model's accuracy can stay at chance while its loss keeps falling. You therefore score the gold answer continuously, in bits per byte, on a benchmark whose signal across models exceeds its noise from checkpoint to checkpoint ",e.jsx(s,{id:"heineman2025signalnoise",children:"Heineman et al., 2025"}),". When you compare models, the protocol is part of the score. Both halves rest on the checklist's rule of comparing only at a quantity held fixed."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The exercise runs both halves in miniature. You train about five small model sizes with WSD decay branches and fit the IsoFLOP minima and then ",e.jsx(a,{math:"N^*(C)",display:!1,trail:"."})," You then predict the loss of a reference model trained with about ten times the compute of your largest run. Finally, you measure models with lm-eval-harness pinned at one version, under two setup ablations, and record the protocol next to every score."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Week 9 uses the same controlled loop to decide which data reaches training."})]})]}),e.jsx(n,{title:"To-dos until next week",accent:2,number:"49",children:e.jsxs("div",{className:"flex flex-col gap-10",children:[e.jsx("div",{className:"flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-x-24 gap-y-6",children:e.jsxs("figure",{className:"text-center",children:[e.jsx("a",{href:"https://tally.so/r/0V876Q",children:e.jsx("img",{src:"images/qr-week-07-muddiest-point.svg",alt:"QR code for the muddiest-point survey, tally.so/r/0V876Q",className:"w-56 h-56"})}),e.jsxs("figcaption",{children:[e.jsx("strong",{children:"Muddiest point"}),e.jsx("br",{}),"Do it right now! (2 min)"]})]})}),e.jsxs("div",{className:"grid gap-x-12 gap-y-6 md:grid-cols-3",children:[e.jsxs("div",{children:[e.jsx("div",{className:"font-semibold",children:"Before the tutorial"}),e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
