`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Your lab's WSD sweep trains each model size to eight token endpoints, at 1/8, 2/8, …, 8/8 of the longest run. Each decay branch costs 20% of the compute needed to reach its endpoint, which is the stable prefix up to the branch plus the branch itself. A colleague says WSD makes the sweep eight times cheaper than training each endpoint as its own cosine run. What is the actual saving? What limits it as you add endpoints, and what would you change to save more?"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"A model's release blog reports 71 on MMLU-Pro. You evaluate the same released weights with your own harness and get 65. Assume that nobody cheated. List the plausible explanations from most to least likely, with a reason for your order."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"You can train a 70B model on 1.4T tokens or an 8B model on 15T tokens. Assume that both models reach the same quality. Training costs about 6ND FLOPs, and generating one token costs about 2N FLOPs. How many tokens must you generate in deployment before the 8B model's extra training compute is paid back by its cheaper generation? Which assumption in this calculation is the weakest?"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"You want a benchmark for automated code review: a model reads a proposed code change and writes review comments. The benchmark should reflect the real task and be cheap to score. Sketch one. The iron rule says: anything goes on the training set, competitive ranking on the test set. Where in your design is this rule hardest to keep, and why?"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Two models answer the same 40 problems, once each. Model A solves six problems that model B misses. Model B solves four problems that A misses. On the other 30 problems, both are right or both are wrong. By how many percentage points does A lead? How strong is the evidence that A is the better model?"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.em,{children:"(week-6 interleave)"})," Your sweep trains five model sizes, from about 2M to 50M parameters. Each size gets one stable-prefix run and four decay branches, 25 jobs in all. The reference model whose loss you predict has 150M parameters and trains as one run. Assume H100 GPUs with 80 GB each. Which parallelism would you use for the sweep, and which for the reference run? Why does Week 6's taxonomy give different answers for the two?"]}),`
`]})})})]}),`
